---
// Fact-forward pass (matches the corrected helical-piers-deep-dive.astro
// treatment) -- re-verified against MFRC's foam-jacking page and rewritten
// in TLS's own structure. Added a real, verified nuance MFRC's page had
// that this one didn't: in documented conditions, foam isn't only a
// void-fill/lift tool -- deeper injection can improve loose or poorly
// compacted soil, same ground-improvement category as compaction
// grouting, just a different material. No misconceptions/Q&A section
// (dropped sitewide). Corrected per Tim: an earlier draft said TLS
// brings in a trusted partner for smaller foam/mud jacking jobs -- that
// was background context for understanding, not something to publish.
// The page now talks about foam the same way every other method page
// talks about TLS's own work. Also strengthened the "opinions vary"
// section per Tim: soil type is the real variable that decides whether
// foam works, not contractor opinion. [TIM: ...] marks spots needing
// real NM numbers/field experience instead of guesses.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Foam Injection in New Mexico | TLS Foundations';
const summary =
  'Polyurethane foam injection fills voids and lifts concrete by expansion force, in small controlled stages -- plenty for a sidewalk or light slab, but not a substitute for piers under a structure carrying real structural load. It is not a pile, and it does not explain why support was lost in the first place; if the underlying cause -- water, erosion, poor compaction -- is still active, foam by itself does not fix that.';
const description =
  'What polyurethane foam injection does, why it cures fast enough to lift a slab in controlled stages, and when it is -- and is not -- the right call.';

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Foam Injection in New Mexico',
    description,
    about: description,
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TLS Foundations' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TLS Foundations' },
    url: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/foam-injection-deep-dive',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Foundation Repair Methods', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/foundation-repair-methods' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Foam Injection', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/foam-injection-deep-dive' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  footer="minimal"
  schema={schema}
  summary={summary}
  pageName="Foam Injection"
  nextPage={{ path: '/foundation-repair-methods', name: 'Foundation Repair Methods' }}
>
  <div class="tls-article" id="top">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero-inner">
        <span class="hero-eyebrow">Methods · Foam Injection</span>
        <h1>Foam Injection —<br /><em>Fast, Precise, and Not a Pile</em></h1>
        <p class="page-hero-sub">Expanding polyurethane lifts by expansion force and cures in minutes. That speed is exactly what makes it good at one job and the wrong tool for another.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-outer">
      <article class="article-body">
        <p class="article-lede">Foam gets a mixed reputation in this industry — some contractors treat it as a cure-all, others write it off almost entirely. Neither extreme holds up. What foam actually does, and where it stops being the right tool, comes down to specifics most of that debate skips.</p>

        <div class="article-section" id="what-they-are">
          <h2>Expansion Force, <em>Not a Pile</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Foam injection uses a two-component polyurethane that expands after it's injected — commonly used to fill voids, restore contact beneath slabs, and lift concrete in small, controlled stages.</p>
          <p>It's not a helical pier, a push pier, or a micropile. It doesn't install a steel or grouted pile — it places expanding resin where there's a void or treatable ground, then relies on the foam and the soil around it to carry load after injection. That puts foam in the same category as compaction grouting rather than piering: it improves or restores what's already there instead of making a physical connection to bearing material. Physical connection is usually the stronger repair when it's achievable; foam earns its place when it isn't, or when the problem genuinely is shallow-to-intermediate voiding rather than a missing deep load path.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="how-they-work">
          <h2>Fast Cure, and <em>Its Real Ceiling</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">The foam expands and cures in minutes, not days — which is what makes it good for precise, incremental lifting: inject a little, check the level, inject a little more, and the surface is often walkable the same day.</p>
          <p>That speed is also its ceiling. If weak, wet, eroding, or compressible soil remains below or beside the treated zone, movement can continue regardless of how well the injection itself went — foam restores contact and fills the void that exists right now, it doesn't change what's happening underneath it. Poor drainage and saturated soil are exactly the conditions that can bring the same settlement back.</p>
          <p>Void filling and slab lift is the usual job, but it isn't the only one. In documented conditions — where the treatable zone and soil type are actually known — deeper injection can also improve loose or poorly compacted soil and support part of a foundation, the same way compaction grouting does, just with a different material. That only applies when the building doesn't need a separate deep load path; it's not a way around one.</p>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Not interchangeable with mud jacking</span>
            <p>Foam is plenty for a sidewalk or a light slab, but it isn't a substitute for piers under a structure carrying real structural load. It's also not the same tool as mud jacking, even though both can lift a slab — mud jacking uses the same cement-based grout as compaction grouting, foam is an expanding polyurethane resin, and cost and cure time differ accordingly. The foam-versus-piers question isn't a product competition — it's a question about what's actually failing and what load path the structure needs.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="why-opinions-vary">
          <h2>Why Opinions on Foam <em>Vary So Much</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Ask five different contractors about foam injection and you'll likely get five different answers — some treat it as the answer to nearly everything, others dismiss it outright as something that never really works.</p>
          <p>Like most things in this industry, the truth sits somewhere in the middle, and the variable that decides it isn't opinion — it's soil type. Foam's effectiveness depends on what the soil is actually doing: how it responds to the injection, how deep the real problem goes, and whether the treatable zone is even known. Foam and chemical grouting both matter for the same underlying reason — they're typically less intrusive and less expensive than a full piering job. But that upside only holds if the soil is actually understood first. Used on the wrong soil type or at the wrong depth, either one can mask a symptom instead of fixing it — which is exactly why the soil comes before the method, not the other way around.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="scope">
          <h2>What Rules Foam <em>Out</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">A footing that still needs reliable structural load transfer to deeper competent material.</p>
          <p>Water or erosion still actively removing support. A job where the soil and movement mechanism haven't actually been identified yet.</p>
        </div>

        <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>

        <p class="closing-link"><a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="body-link">← Back to Foundation Repair Methods</a> · <a href="/glossary#polyurethane-foam-injection" class="body-link">Glossary: Polyurethane Foam Injection →</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

